leaving 4 to go to children. But children sizes are 4 and 2 -> 6. Inconsistency indicates either the numbers are symbolic and not required to be consistent (the problem likely expects us to compute stability from these fields regardless of numeric consistency), or we need to compute the stability for each cluster as sum over point_exit_lambdas of (lambda_exit - birth_lambda). And then for the cluster selection, we use a bottom-up tree traversal to compare stability of a node with the sum of stabilities of its selected children. That is the standard HDBSCAN algorithm.\n\nGiven the output schema, we need to return clusters with cluster_id, size, stability. Stability is a number. We'll compute stability for each cluster. Then we need to select clusters based on the hierarchy. The output also includes `selected_cluster_ids`, which are the IDs of clusters that are chosen by the algorithm.\n\nThus, the algorithm:\n- Build a dictionary of clusters keyed by cluster_id, with fields: size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas (list), parent (from the parent mapping, can be None).\n- Compute stability for each cluster: sum over all exit lambdas of (exit_lambda - birth_lambda). Note: if point_exit_lambdas is empty? Then stability is 0.\n- Then perform bottom-up selection: For each cluster, compute the total stability of its subtree if we select its children (i.e., sum of stabilities of selected nodes in its descendant subtree). Actually, the HDBSCAN algorithm does a post-order traversal. For each node, we compare its own stability with the sum of the stabilities of its children's subtrees (if children are selected). If node.stability >= sum(child selected stabilities), then we select this node and deselect all descendants. Otherwise, we keep the children selected and don't select the node. The root is not selected (or is selected? Typically root is the whole set, often not selected because it's too large. But algorithm: we traverse starting from leaves; for each node, we compute the sum of the stabilities of its child clusters (the selected ones from its descendants). If the node's stability is greater than or equal to that sum, we set this node as selected and mark its descendants as not selected. The root is not selected because it's too large? Actually in standard HDBSCAN, the root is the whole dataset, but it's not a final cluster. So we can treat the root as having no parent (parent is None), and after traversal, we select the clusters that are marked as selected.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Create a map from cluster_id to cluster data.\n2. Find root(s): clusters with parent None (usually one root).\n3. Build a tree structure: for each cluster, children list.\n4. Post-order traversal (recursive or iterative) to compute selection.\n   - For a leaf (no children): it is selected initially? Actually, leaves are baseline selected. But then we compare leaf with its parent. Typically algorithm: for each node, compute the sum of stabilities of its selected descendant clusters. Then if node's stability >= that sum, mark node as selected and deselect descendants. For a leaf, its selected descendant sum is 0 (since no children). So if leaf.stability >= 0, it selects itself. Then when processing parent, it sums the stabilities of its selected children (which may be leaves). So that works.\n\n   Implementation: function process_node(node_id):\n       node = clusters[node_id]\n       total_child_selected_stability = sum(process_node(child) for child in node.children)\n       if node.stability >= total_child_selected_stability:\n           # select this node, deselect descendants\n           node.selected = True\n           for child in node.children:\n               node.selected_descendants = False (or remove from final set)\n           return node.stability\n       else:\n           # keep children selected, don't select node\n           node.selected = False\n           return total_child_selected_stability\n\n   But careful: we need to collect selected cluster IDs at the end. We can use a set or list.\n\n   More standard approach: do post-order, and for each node compute a value \"best_stability\" = max(node.stability, sum_child_best). If node.stability >= sum_child_best, then node is selected, and we remove children from selection. We can implement by returning a tuple (selected_set, stability). Or we can do a two-pass: first compute the best stability, then propagate selections. Simpler: recursion returns a boolean indicating if node is selected, and also compute the sum of selected child stabilities. Or we can use a global set and manipulate.\n\n   Actually, we can do:\n   def solve_node(node_id):\n       node = clusters[node_id]\n       if not node.children:\n           # leaf\n           selected_set.add(node_id)\n           return node.stability\n       child_sum = 0\n       for child in node.children:\n           child_sum += solve_node(child)\n       if node.stability >= child_sum:\n           # select this node, deselect all children\n           selected_set.add(node_id)\n           for child in node.children:\n               selected_set.discard(child)  # but child may have been selected recursively; need to remove all descendants? However, if we built set as we go, we might need to remove them. Easier: we can just not add children to set initially and use a different approach: first, collect all leaf or all nodes? Better to have a bottom-up selection that returns the set of selected clusters in the subtree. Then at each node, we compute own stability and the children's selected stabilities.\n\n   Pseudocode:\n   def compute(node_id):\n       node = clusters[node_id]\n       if not node.children:\n           # leaf: return (selected set = {node_id}, total_stability = node.stability)\n           return ({node_id}, node.stability)\n       else:\n           child_results = [compute(child) for child in node.children]\n           child_sets = [r[0] for r in child_results]\n           child_stabilities = [r[1] for r in child_results]\n           total_child_stab = sum(child_stabilities)\n           if node.stability >= total_child_stab:\n               # select this node, deselect children sets\n               new_set = {node_id}\n               # children sets are discarded\n               return (new_set, node.stability)\n           else:\n               # keep children's selections\n               union_set = set()\n               for s in child_sets:\n                   union_set.update(s)\n               return (union_set, total_child_stab)\n\n   Start from root. At root, we get a set of selected clusters. But note: root may be selected or not. Typically, the root (the whole dataset) is not considered a valid cluster because it's too large and unstable. The algorithm often excludes the root. Looking at the fixture, the payload has a parent mapping from c0, c1, c2. c0 parent null, so root is c0. c0 has size 7. c1 and c2 are its children. The algorithm should decide whether to select c0 or its children. We need to compute stability.\n\n   Compute stability for c0: exit lambdas [0.4,0.6,0.9] -> sum (0.4-0.2)=0.2, (0.6-0.2)=0.4, (0.9-0.2)=0.7 => total 1.3.\n   c1: exit lambdas [0.7,1.0,1.2] -> (0.7-0.5)=0.2, (1.0-0.5)=0.5, (1.2-0.5)=0.7 => total 1.4.\n   c2: exit lambdas [1.1] -> (1.1-0.8)=0.3 => total 0.3.\n   Now children of c0 are c1 and c2. Sum of children stabilities = 1.4+0.3=1.7. c0 stability 1.3 < 1.7, so we keep children. Thus selected cluster IDs should be {c1, c2}. That seems plausible. For the fixture, the outputs might be as such. The problem doesn't give expected outputs, but we can infer from the task description that this is the HDBSCAN cluster selection.\n\n   However, need to check the definition of stability: In HDBSCAN, stability is sum over points of (lambda_max - lambda_min). But here, the point_exit_lambdas are the lambdas at which points leave the cluster. For a leaf cluster, points that leave are those that eventually become noise. But in